Let \( c \) and \( b \) represent the number of pounds of corn and beans Logan buys, respectively. We can set up the following system of equations based on the given information:
\begin{align*}
b + c &= 30 \\
1.20c + 0.60b &= 27
\end{align*}
Multiply the first equation by 0.60 to facilitate elimination:
\begin{align*}
0.60(b + c) &= 0.60 \times 30 \\
0.60b + 0.60c &= 18
\end{align*}
Now, subtract this equation from the second equation:
\begin{align*}
1.20c + 0.60b - (0.60b + 0.60c) &= 27 - 18 \\
0.60c &= 9
\end{align*}
Solving for \( c \):
\begin{align*}
c &= \frac{9}{0.60} \\
c &= 15
\end{align*}
Therefore, Logan buys \( \boxed{15.0 \text{ pounds}} \) of corn.

